{
  "requirement": "Borrow the identity of the source node being checked.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<'d, D: Domain> ValidationContext<'d, D> {\n    pub fn node_id(&self) -> &D::NodeId;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement `requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.ValidationContext.node_id` has one obligation. `node_id` must return a borrow (`&D::NodeId`) of the identity of the source node the context was built for.\n\nThe marked test `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/validation.rs#fn[labeled_selections_track_only_target_membership_without_reading_contents]` builds `ValidationContext::new(&graph, 1)` and asserts `ctx.node_id() == &1`. The same test also queries other nodes through the context (`outgoing_edges(&9, 7)` and `successors(&7)`). So the assertion shows `node_id` returns the context's own source node, not a queried target. Because `NodeId` is a generic type, an implementation can't invent a value. It can only return a borrow of the stored identity. The assertion therefore rules out wrong-field and wrong-node behavior, and the test shows the return type is a reference.\n\nOther tests use `node_id` in practice, for example `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/validation.rs` `impl[Memory]/fn[run_review]` and `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs`. They are not marked, but they add no obligation.\n\nThe requirement names no failure or boundary cases. The `public_exports_match_the_appendix` test only covers the public API inventory, which belongs to other requirements. I found no adequacy gap.",
